## Native ordering acceptance

The accounted source suite includes the one new Linux acceptance test in
`tests/test_sft_release_gate_linux_v1.py`. On the Ubuntu runner its sole platform
skip guard does not apply, and it requires a zero-exit structural report from
the privileged fixture. The 70 added passing tests correspond to 46 gate tests,
23 source tests and this one native test; skipped count remains 58. The job emits
aggregate/quiet results, not a separately retained per-test PASS line or raw
fixture stdout. Native claims below are the assertions required by that wrapper,
not a fabricated standalone fixture transcript.

The four preserved positive lifecycle cases now verify the declared generated
tree before the guardian's original callback may send `b"G"`. Actual system
CPython 3.12 preflight is enforced, with no verifier runtime-guard patch. The
accepted IPC/filesystem/Landlock/privilege/cleanup path and CPU WorkerRun retain
three invocations, six rank workers, four global and eight rank optimizer updates.

Two additional fresh blocked-WorkerRun cases reject a wrong external manifest pin
and a same-size generated dependency tamper. Each requires one failed verification
attempt and **zero original release attempts/completions**. Their one blocked
worker is SIGKILL-reaped, their exact controller is reaped with exit status 2,
and their owned cgroup is confirmed empty and removed. Retirement is counted
once; descriptor identities balance before and after the watcher.

The experiment totals **six cgroups and six generated trees removed**. The two
negative cases additionally require two seccomp installations and four exposed
socket removals; the original four-case positive counters remain separate.
No negative WorkerRun invocation follows from the verified zero release calls
and frozen wait-for-G-before-exec sequence. This is not presented as an independent
exec instrumentation counter, nor is the guardian's callback-consumption flag
misrepresented as proof that G was sent.

The final aggregate report is bounded to 4096 bytes. Generated manifest/content
limits, exact cleanup targets, false nonclaims and threat-model premises are
recorded in v0.47. The three 106-byte stand-ins are explicitly **not the runtime
executing WorkerRun**. No coherent snapshot, execution immutability, complete
dynamic dependency closure, publisher authentication or real-host admission is
claimed. Real SFT execution and production eligibility remain false.

